A||||4||fierospeeder||Bearings Change?||||5
Z||000000||fierospeeder||11-19-1999||04:57 PM||||While im doing my oilpan AGAIN.  This is after a year.  I was wondering is it possible to change my piston/crank bearings.  If anyone has done this.  2.5L 4cylinder.  Such the 4 bearings that hold the pistons is all i need because i know its worn out.  Bringing the piston to the lowest point (downstroke) taking the bolts out raising the piston (spark plug out)  slapping on some new bearings pulling the piston down applying lock tight, retorquing.  Cranking the engine to the next Piston rod?  <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||11-19-1999||05:09 PM||||no, it's not rocket science.......<P>Putting new bearings on an old crank without, at the very least, polishing the journals is a good way to spin a bearing. Spinning a bearing can do some pretty nasty things to the motor. nasty to the point of snapping a rod and pushing it thru the block.<P>If your crank is on decent shape you can have that work done at many engine shops fairly reasonably. You then of course replace the whole bearing set.<P>It is posible to do all this without the head off, but not recomended. Also you must not allow the rod or any other object to contact the journals or you  will likely mess one up. Only the new, OILED, bearings must touch the journals. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||11-21-1999||04:32 AM||||how crucial is it to bring the engine to a shop because i know a lot of being that do rebuilds just hone the cylinder walls and do the ridge reamer.  Slap new bearings do all new rings and put the engine back in the car.  Without having to go to the machine shop.  Right now my bearings were worn out when i bought the car and im using the cheap way of using thick oil.  I dont have the time or money to do a rebuild now because the car isn't on top of my list, i got another project car i got to finish.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-21-1999||12:22 PM||||If the motor is worn that bad, then likely the whole thing need rebuild. If the motor is fine other wise, no you don't have to rebuild. <P>You can change the crank w/o machining the whole motor. You need to have the crank changed or machined before puting new bearings on it. The new bearing will not last long if you don't. Bearing failure can ruin the whole motor.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tim||11-21-1999||05:51 PM||||I've fitted rod and main bearings as a temperory fix before. Just know before hand that it is a patch job and needs to be done right.  The last car I did was a mopar 2.2 turbo.  I got two years and about 25K out of fitting rod bearings to a spun rod.||||reg||1
